<p>A New Mexico jury found Meta liable for violating state law by misleading users about platform safety and endangering children on Facebook and Instagram. After roughly a day of deliberations following a nearly seven-week trial, jurors ordered the company to pay $375 million in civil penalties. State lawyers argued that Meta failed to protect minors from sexual abuse, online solicitation, human trafficking, and broader mental-health harms, and jurors concluded that the company’s conduct violated New Mexico’s Unfair Practices Act. The ruling matters because several outlets described it as the first time a US state has won a trial against a major tech company over harm to young users, which could strengthen similar cases moving through US courts. New Mexico had sought as much as $2.2 billion, but the jury chose a smaller penalty. Meta said it disagrees with the verdict and plans to appeal, while maintaining that it works to remove harmful content and bad actors from its platforms.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/WhSrv4iEvLKpsBm-xYDGFVBuJaX4j74ql_9-nv4xiDWgIThY0nVzvIPz_gAJOZXvDopB2gKxsgGja4IYOhZhdF1mBy84S2IO-nt9A9STpV72mroAsRLLNeAwIgjGBs-gZ73IPFmrT7XqvRfjusF2L_1JMFFipr10Pt3IxWkTImOyQYk6mYoe2X_bgObp-kqV9yGOG9O3tESJhIIPFtvPUV67i-87nsZ-yvvFmnpF1U0nirFlw3VJRRi-h0iipm_j4Cy9YJRhQEpaLRd_Ag' alt='A recording of Meta founder and CEO Mark Zuckerberg&#x27;s deposition is played for jurors during the New Mexico trial in Santa Fe.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cross-state origins: Politico reported that the New Mexico case grew out of a Nevada Department of Justice investigation into Meta’s safeguards against the sexual abuse of minors, showing how scrutiny spread before this trial began.</li><li>California spillover: A separate federal case over whether Meta and YouTube are defective products that harm children was already in jury deliberations in California, so another closely watched youth-safety ruling could come soon.</li><li>Litigation playbook: Politico reported that regulators and families are increasingly using products-liability claims against social media firms to test platform responsibility despite Section 230 protections for third-party content.</li><li>Bellwether question: DW described the outcome as a possible bellwether because the liability finding could shape other youth-safety cases, even though the penalty was only a fraction of what New Mexico sought.</li></ul><blockquote>Meta executives knew their products harmed children, disregarded warnings from their own employees, and lied to the public about what they knew - Raúl Torrez</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>New Mexico attorney general Raúl Torrez: Torrez called the verdict proof that jurors sided with families, educators, and child-safety experts, casting the case as a state effort to hold a major platform accountable under consumer-protection law. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/24/meta-hit-with-375m-verdict-in-new-mexico-child-safety-case-00842998'>POLITICO</a>)</li><li>Meta: Meta argued that policing its services is an ongoing challenge because harmful content and bad actors can be hard to spot and remove, and said it already puts substantial effort into those safety tasks across its platforms. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/24/meta-hit-with-375m-verdict-in-new-mexico-child-safety-case-00842998'>POLITICO</a>, <a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cql75dn07n2o'>BBC</a>)</li><li>New Mexico state government: State officials cast the case as a broader test of whether social platforms can market themselves as safe for families while allegedly failing to back up those promises with effective protections for minors. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/24/meta-new-mexico-jury'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/technology/science/article-meta-trial-new-mexico-children-mental-health/'>Meta knowingly harmed children’s mental health and prioritized profits over safety, jury determines</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347773/meta-ordered-pay-us375-million-over-child-exploitation-and-user-safety-claims'>Meta ordered to pay US$375 million in New Mexico child safety trial</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/24/meta-new-mexico-jury'>Meta ordered to pay $375m after being found liable in child exploitation case</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/inthenews/comments/1s2toln/new_mexico_jury_says_meta_harms_childrens_mental/'>New Mexico jury says Meta harms children&#x27;s mental health and safety, violating state law</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/meta-hit-with-375-million-fine-in-child-exploitation-case/a-76515470'>Meta hit with $375 million fine in child exploitation case</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/24/meta-hit-with-375m-verdict-in-new-mexico-child-safety-case-00842998'>Meta hit with $375M verdict in New Mexico child safety case</a> - politico.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/new-mexico-jury-says-meta-harms-childrens-mental-health-and-safety-violating-state-law/article70782437.ece'>New Mexico jury says Meta harms children’s mental health and safety, violating state law</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/meta-ordered-to-pay-375-million-over-child-exploitation-user-safety-claims-11262371#publisher=newsstand'>Meta Ordered To Pay $375 Million Over Child Exploitation, User Safety Claims</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/3/25/us-jury-orders-meta-to-pay-375m-for-endangering-children'>US jury orders Meta to pay $375m for endangering children</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cql75dn07n2o'>Meta told to pay $375m for misleading users over child safety</a> - bbc.co